#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096437 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/id #uuid "b58fbe0d-bab7-57b3-931f-ef8d4e1990c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5634" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ and $P(A\\cup B) = \\dfrac{4}{9}$, find $P(B)$." :question/worked-solution "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) \\\\\nP(B) &= P(A∪B) - P(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{9} - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 3}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096438 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "711b6630-ea78-53f6-b830-6ecaac9a468a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5636" :question/problem "Two cards are drawn from a standard $52$-card deck: the ace of hearts and the queen of hearts. These are set apart, and a third card is drawn from the remaining deck. What is the probability that the third drawn card is an ace or a heart?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that the third card is an ace, and let $H$ be the event that the third card is a heart. Then the probability that the third card is an ace or a heart can be represented as $P(A\\cup H)$.\n\nGiven that the ace of hearts is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $H$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e.,\n\n$$\nP(A \\cap H) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can find $P(A)$ and $P(H)$ by computing the necessary ratios. When the third card is drawn, there are $50$ cards in the deck. Among the remaining $50$ cards, there are $13-2=11$ hearts and $4-1=3$ aces. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{3}{50} \\\\\nP(H) &= \\frac{11}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪H) &= P(A) + P(H) - P(A∩H) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{50} + \\frac{11}{50} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{50} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65bec174-0235-5a32-a0d2-2892af4ddaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Events are Mutually Exclusive"}] :question/id #uuid "a968e5dc-f9fc-5bf7-8879-5276e0512483" :question/math-academy-id "e-5727" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\dfrac{1}{3}$, $P(B)=\\dfrac{1}{4}$, and $P(A\\cup B)=\\dfrac{7}{12}$, determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = 0\n$$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A∪B) &= P(A) + P(B) - P(A∩B) \\\\\n\\frac{7}{12} &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - P(A∩B) \\\\\nP(A∩B) &= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{7}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 + 3 - 7}{12} \\\\\n&= 0✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fa2a25d-f148-50e4-bffa-d7475e590812" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a039349e-409c-53c9-807e-87652659e7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094257} {:answer/id #uuid "b374a0bd-bad0-5861-b51a-9b4c63b48597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{23}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094258} {:answer/id #uuid "0d21f686-73b9-52bb-b0dd-621e0e31ef77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094259} {:answer/id #uuid "24ba5170-a5b7-5d3c-997a-9d8e3e378db9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094260} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc3ea7-bfde-55fb-8f14-7f050b9b991a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a039349e-409c-53c9-807e-87652659e7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094257} :answer-field/id #uuid "13c1a5de-4031-5d1c-bdf4-13af166d4e70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094256}] :question/id #uuid "1b8787c0-54a9-5103-b996-02111db7628d" :question/math-academy-id "q-12551" :question/problem "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A) = \\frac{2}{7}$ and $P(A∪B) = \\frac{3}{4}$, find $P(B)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d7ee11-f78e-572b-83c4-5dbbe52dc1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095804} {:answer/id #uuid "2178839c-8153-53b8-a6fd-c73ee460f34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095805} {:answer/id #uuid "35f50752-0af2-55b8-b9f8-746e947acd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095806} {:answer/id #uuid "6debf44b-a834-58cd-a2ca-73a7e7b522b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095807} {:answer/id #uuid "70e06785-16b7-50d5-bc3e-9ed2f7d82c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/id